<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Clemens Brentano</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1778">1778</a>–<a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Klemens Brentano, Clemens Maria Brentano, Clemens Wenzeslaus Brentano</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Clemens Brentano was a German poet and novelist and a major figure of German <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>, born into a wealthy merchant family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> and brother of the writer Bettina von Arnim. With Achim von Arnim he collected the folk songs of Des Knaben Wunderhorn (1805–1808), which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler">Gustav Mahler</a> later drew upon. In 1818 he returned to the Catholic faith and until 1824 took down at Dülmen the visions of the nun Anne Catherine Emmerich, published as The Dolorous Passion of Our Lord <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus Christ</a> in 1833, though later investigations made it uncertain how much was his own. His works also include the romance Godwi (1801) and the Romanzen vom Rosenkranz, published in 1852; he died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg">Aschaffenburg</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/clemens-brentano.jpg" alt="Clemens Brentano" width="330" height="489" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Emilie Linder · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Clemens_Brentano2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Clemens Brentano was born on 9 September 1778 into a wealthy merchant family in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a>, the son of Peter Anton Brentano and Maximiliane von La Roche. His maternal grandmother was the writer Sophie von La Roche, and his sister Bettina von Arnim corresponded with Goethe as a young girl and published the letters in 1835. He studied in Halle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a> and lived afterwards at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a>. He was close to Wieland, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottfried-herder">Herder</a>, Goethe, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/friedrich-schlegel">Friedrich Schlegel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-gottlieb-fichte">Fichte</a> and Tieck.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1798 to 1800 he lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jena">Jena</a>, the first centre of the Romantic movement, and in 1801 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gottingen">Göttingen</a>, where he befriended Achim von Arnim. In 1804 he moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> and worked with Arnim on Des Knaben Wunderhorn, a collection of folk songs published from 1805 to 1808 on which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler">Gustav Mahler</a> later drew. In 1818 he returned to the practice of the Catholic faith and withdrew to Dülmen, where he became secretary to the visionary nun Anne Catherine Emmerich, copying her dictation until her death in 1824.</p><p>He then prepared an index of her visions, The Dolorous Passion of Our Lord Jesus Christ, published in 1833. Investigations in 1923 and 1928 made it uncertain how much of the books he attributed to her was his own creation. He spent his later years in Regensburg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, promoting the Catholic faith.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Brentano belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg">Heidelberg</a> group of German Romantic writers, and his works are marked by fantastic imagery and abrupt, bizarre modes of expression. His first publications were Satiren und poetische Spiele (1800) and the romance Godwi (1801). His finest work is generally held to be the Romanzen vom Rosenkranz, published posthumously in 1852, and his short story Geschichte vom braven Kasperl und dem schönen Annerl (1817) was very popular. His collected works, edited by his brother Christian, appeared in nine volumes from 1851 to 1855. He died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg">Aschaffenburg</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Clemens Brentano” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Achim von Arnim and <b>Clemens Brentano</b> gathered German songs in The Boy’s Magic Horn from 1805, and the Brothers Grimm their tales from 1812.</blockquote><a href="/movement/romanticism">Romanticism · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>An early version, a dialogue between a prisoner in a tower and his beloved, was printed as &quot;Song of the Persecuted in the Tower, after Swiss songs&quot; in Achim von Arnim and <b>Clemens Brentano</b>'s folk-poetry collection Des Knaben Wunderhorn, about 1805.</blockquote><a href="/songs/die-gedanken-sind-frei">Die Gedanken sind frei</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1801 <b>Clemens Brentano</b> wrote the ballad &quot;Zu Bacharach am Rheine&quot;, part of a fragmentary continuation of his novel Godwi.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lorelei">Die Lorelei</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Koblenz-Ehrenbreitstein, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aschaffenburg">Aschaffenburg</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.36,7.62&amp;year=1819" aria-label="Koblenz-Ehrenbreitstein on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4724 414 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Clemens Brentano"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5174 684h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5174 684h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.36,7.62&amp;year=1819">What was here in 1819? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
